Measure what a real SMS round trip does to a droplet loop

A rendered QR droplet loop went over SMS and came back. This writes down what
survived, what predicts survival, and which encoder defaults I think should
change. Doc only — no code, no default actually changed yet.

**The good news, and it is real:** 584×584 → 192×192, and it still reassembled
the whole 16,358-byte payload with a verified signature in a single pass. Of 234
frames that decoded before and after, all 234 agreed byte for byte. SMS dropped
no frames and changed no timing; the damage was purely spatial, and QR
binarization plus Reed-Solomon ate all of it.

**The finding I did not expect, and the reason this is a doc rather than a
message:** at our 1.5x overhead default, a loop needs ~80% of its frames to
decode or it can never reassemble — the fountain needs ~1.2*K distinct droplets,
so the required decode rate is 1.2/overhead. SMS delivered 95%, so this one had
room. A worse channel would not, and there is no warning before it stops working.

Two thresholds predict every result I measured:
- px/module >= 2.0 (Nyquist). Above it the stream lives; below it 40 passes
  never reassemble, because the failures are deterministic and looping adds no
  information. It is a cliff, not a slope.
- usable frames >= 1.2 * K. This one caught a case px/module alone gets wrong:
  at 144px, EC L decoded 64.4% (168 frames, under K=174) and died forever, while
  EC M decoded 82.4% (215 frames) and caught in one pass.

Decisive experiment for the recommendation: same payload, same EC, same 144px,
same ~65% decode rate — overhead 1.5x never reassembles, overhead 3x is caught
in one pass and verified.

Recommended (not applied here, they are defaults in the render tool which lives
in the workspace, not this repo): overhead 1.5 -> 2.5, EC M -> L, a ~50-frame
minimum, record minOutputWidth in the manifest, and raise the catch test's miss
rate from 25% to ~40% since it passed everything and did not predict the failure
above.

Verification note: decoding used OpenCV's detector, which at these module sizes
is the limiting factor rather than the damage — the SMS copy scored *higher*
than the pristine original (95.0% vs 93.9%) and their failure sets overlap in 2
frames of 261. So every number here is a floor; a phone does better.

Flagged, unsolved, and worse than anything SMS did: dwell time. At 4fps, K=707
(impact-of-ai) needs 3.5 minutes of continuous filming to catch. Overhead does
not help — dwell scales with payload size. That needs a decision about payload
sizing before anything ships as "point your camera at this".

Raw data, scripts and the SMS sample are in the workspace at
renders/lucky-sevens/_degradation-study/ (FINDINGS.md has the full tables), since
that tree is not a repo.
